+\S2{config-osc52} \q{Remote clipboard writes (OSC 52)}
+
+Some terminal applications, such as \c{tmux}, Vim and Neovim, can copy
+text to the clipboard of the machine you are sitting at, by sending an
+\i{OSC 52} escape sequence. This control decides what PuTTY does with
+such requests:
+
+\b \q{Deny} (the default) ignores them.
+
+\b \q{Ask} shows a dialog describing the request (its size, its number
+of lines and the start of the text) and writes to the clipboard only if
+you confirm. If you refuse, PuTTY stops asking and ignores further
+requests until your next key press or mouse click in the window. (On
+Unix, this mode is not
+yet implemented, and acts like \q{Deny}.)
+
+\b \q{Allow} writes the text to the system clipboard without asking.
+Any program that can write to the terminal can then replace the
+contents of your clipboard.
+
+Only writing is supported. Requests to \e{read} the clipboard are
+ignored and never answered. The text must be valid UTF-8 and at most
+100 KiB long once decoded; malformed or oversized requests are
+silently discarded.
+
